1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,922
Hi. This is Explainer Studio, and today it is explaining itself. You give it some text, and it gives you back a narrated video.

2
00:00:08,662 --> 00:00:17,671
Explainer Studio is a small Windows app. You paste in source text, and it writes a script, draws the slides, records a voice and builds the video.

3
00:00:17,871 --> 00:00:21,424
The writing and the voices can run entirely on your own computer.

4
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:28,366
First, a writing model running on your machine turns the source into a script. Then the slides are drawn from that script.

5
00:00:28,546 --> 00:00:35,199
Next, a voice reads every slide. Finally, FFmpeg joins the pictures and the voice into one video.

6
00:00:35,912 --> 00:00:44,573
There are two kinds of template. Designed templates look at what each slide says, and choose a layout to match, such as steps, a comparison or one big statement.

7
00:00:44,753 --> 00:00:49,060
Classic templates, versus that, keep the same layout on every slide.

8
00:00:49,745 --> 00:00:55,248
The studio works in English, Arabic and Farsi, and right-to-left slides are drawn the right way round.

9
00:00:55,428 --> 00:01:03,091
Every slide ends with a different transition, and each one drifts slowly while it is on screen. If you prefer plain cuts, you can turn that off.

10
00:01:03,787 --> 00:01:09,638
You do not have to trust the draft. The storyboard lets you edit every heading, key point and spoken sentence.

11
00:01:09,818 --> 00:01:14,404
You can set the pause and the feeling of each segment, and render the video when you are ready.

12
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:21,436
That is the whole idea. Paste some text, review the storyboard, and let the studio make the video.

13
00:01:21,636 --> 00:01:22,843
Thanks for watching.
